>>
endobj
749 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 288 0 R
/E (The primary objective of this project is to design and develop an intelligent indoor navigation system that assists users in efficiently navigating academic buildings without relying on GPS or hardware-intensive positioning technologies. The system aims to provide accurate, reliable, and user-friendly navigation support for students, visitors, and staff within campus environments by leveraging software-based solutions.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
750 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 288 0 R
/E (Another key objective is to implement a QR-based access mechanism that allows users to enter the navigation system instantly through a simple scan, eliminating the need for dedicated mobile applications or additional infrastructure. This ensures ease of deployment and accessibility across different devices, including smartphones and tablets, while reducing maintenance complexity and cost.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
751 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 288 0 R
/E (Indoor navigation within academic institutions has become an increasingly important challenge due to the growing size and complexity of campus buildings. New students, visitors, and staff often face difficulties in locating classrooms, laboratories, offices, and other facilities, especially in unfamiliar environments. Unlike outdoor navigation, indoor spaces cannot rely on GPS due to weak or unavailable signals. As a result, traditional aids such as printed maps and static signboards remain in use, despite their limited ability to provide real-time, personalized guidance. Existing indoor navigation systems often depend on hardware-based technologies such as Wi-Fi fingerprinting, Bluetooth beacons, or sensor networks, which increase deployment cost, maintenance effort, and system complexity, making them unsuitable for many academic environments.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
