781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 290 0 R
/T (Hybrid Campus Navigation System: Seamless Integration of  Indoor and Outdoor Routing,)
/E (Hybrid Campus Navigation System: Seamless Integration of  Indoor and Outdoor Routing,)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
782 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 290 0 R
/T (LITERATURESURVEY)
/E (LITERATURESURVEY)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
783 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 290 0 R
/E (Ramamoorthy, Yadav, and Pal proposed a Hybrid Campus Navigation System that integrates both indoor and outdoor routing to provide seamless navigation across a university campus. Their system addresses the limitation of conventional navigation solutions that handle indoor and outdoor spaces separately. Outdoor navigation is supported using GPS-based routing, while indoor navigation relies on digital floor maps and predefined pathways. The integration of these two navigation modes allows users to transition smoothly between outdoor campus areas and indoor buildings without interruption. The authors emphasize accuracy, continuity, and user convenience as the core strengths of their approach. The system demonstrates improved navigation efficiency across large campus environments by combining multiple navigation technologies. However, the proposed solution requires careful coordination between indoor maps and outdoor positioning systems. This work highlights the importance of unified navigation systems and provides valuable insights that motivate the development of lightweight, scalable campus navigation solutions)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 290 0 R
/E (Liu, Darabi, Banerjee, and Liu presented a comprehensive survey of wireless indoor positioning techniques and systems, highlighting the challenges of accurate localization in indoor environments. The study reviews various technologies including Wi-Fi fingerprinting, Bluetooth, RFID, infrared, and ultra-wideband systems. The authors analyze the strengths and limitations of each method in terms of accuracy, cost, scalability, and infrastructure requirements. While many techniques achieve high positioning accuracy, they often depend on extensive hardware deployment, signal calibration, and maintenance. The survey emphasizes that environmental changes such as walls, obstacles, and signal interference significantly affect system performance. This work provides a foundational understanding of wireless indoor positioning systems and underscores the trade-offs between accuracy and deployment complexity. The findings motivate the need for alternative, software-centric indoor navigation approaches that reduce hardware dependency while maintaining usability and reliability.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
